  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1230 PM CDT Thu Jul 2 2026\n\nMore oppressive heat is on the way today as the strong 500 mb\nridge continues to plague the eastern CONUS with dreadful levels\nof heat and humidity. Morning temperatures have already soared\ninto the low 90s across the region, which are expected to climb\nto between 95 F and 100 F through this evening. Even with ample\ndiurnal mixing, dewpoints will remain above 70 F in most places,\nleading to heat indices well above 105 F. Most of the region,\napart from northeastern Mississippi, are likely to see heat\nindices climb just above 110 F. As such, we have continued the\nExtreme Heat Warning for these areas while maintaining a Heat\nAdvisory for northeastern Mississippi.\n\nThe weak upper system that aided in the development of yesterday\nevenings' thunderstorms has managed to make it just a bit further\nnorthwest through the night. Water vapor imagery now places it\nover Middle Tennessee with a growing cumulus field by 17z. This\nfollows a similar pattern to what preceded thunderstorm\ninitiation yesterday where increasingly agitated cumulus develops\nover higher terrain, eventually growing into convective clusters\nthat travel west. The 12z Nashville upper air data shows very\nsteep mid-level lapse rates in excess of 8 C/km, CAPE exceeding\n3500 - 4000 J/kg, and effective bulk shear values between 10 -\n20 knots. This parameter space, in conjunction with DCAPE >\n1200 J/kg, will lead to another day of powerful thunderstorms\ncapable of damaging microbursts. Thunderstorms are expected to\nform just east of the Cumberland Plateau through this afternoon,\nbringing this threat of damaging winds west into portions of West\nTennessee near the Tennessee River and northeastern Mississippi.\nOutflow from these thunderstorms should make it to the\nMississippi River this evening, quickly bringing temperatures\ndown from their peak in the middle to upper 90s overnight.\nTherefore, the Extreme Heat Warning will end at 8 p.m. with an\narea-wide Heat Advisory taking its place thereafter.\n\nAlthough temperatures may cool somewhat overnight along and east\nof the Mississippi River, high humidity will remain. This will\nallow for temperatures to return to the middle to upper 90s\nFriday alongside heat indices in between 105 F - 109 F. Again,\nafternoon and evening thunderstorms are possible, but forcing\nwill be nebulous and likely dependent on today's convective\nevolution. So, forecast confidence in the exact severity of\nthunderstorms tomorrow is low at this time. However, similar\nconditions to Wednesday and today are likely to still be in place\notherwise, which would lend some credibility to a damaging wind\nthreat if storms are to develop. Any storms will impact the\nextent to which afternoon heat indices are able to climb and has\nkept us from leaning towards warmer guidance that would suggest\nthe need for an Extreme Heat Warning and instead kept the area in\nan area-wide Heat Advisory Friday.\n\nThis diurnal thunderstorm and oppressive heat pattern will\ncontinue through the holiday weekend. Confidence is high enough\nto maintain the area-wide heat advisory again on the 4th of July\nwith heat indices between 105 F - 109 F and potential afternoon\nshowers and thunderstorms. Forecast uncertainty will have\nballooned at this point in the forecast given two days of\nconvective feedback into the forecast. So, it is nearly\nimpossible to pin down any exact locations that would be impacted\nby thunderstorms during holiday celebrations. Sunday will be much\nof the same, but the ridge will begin to slightly weaken with\nafternoon heat indices beginning to drop near 105 F across the\nregion. With a weakening ridge and the potential for afternoon\noutflow from thunderstorms, we have opted to not extend this\nweekend's Heat Advisory into Sunday and will wait until future\nforecast periods as the forecast becomes clearer.\n\nBy Monday, most guidance shows the 500 mb ridge weakening below\n594 dm and high temperatures falling to near 90 F. While some\nareas, particularly the Mississippi River Delta in eastern\nArkansas and northwest Mississippi, could still reach the 105 F\nheat index needed for a heat advisory, most of the region will\nfall below this level. That being said, the heat may not be less\noppressive as the current forecast calls for the rest of the\nregion to have triple digit heat indices through the remainder of\nthe forecast. Lastly, with lower upper heights and high surface\nhumidity, afternoon showers and storms are expected each day\nthrough the end of the period.",
